{"title":"2011 AIME I Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Cyclic_Residues_and_Equivalencies)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Cyclic Residues and Equivalencies)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The vertices of a regular nonagon (9-sided polygon) are to be labeled with the digits 1 through 9 in such a way that the sum of the numbers on every three consecutive vertices is a multiple of 3.  Two acceptable arrangements are considered to be indistinguishable if one can be obtained from the other by rotating the nonagon in the plane.  Find the number of distinguishable acceptable arrangements.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Photo_for_2010_AIME_5.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/4/40/Photo_for_2010_AIME_5.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"578\" height=\"528\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Red <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s mean numbers divisible by 3, green <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s mean remainder of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> when divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>~WhatdoHumanitariansEat\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, we determine which possible combinations of digits <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> will yield sums that are multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. It is simplest to do this by looking at each of the digits <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/f/7ffb54dae2d6238385a61cce8b447c54e319d733.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bmod{3}$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>We see that the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/6041a3a231da612ca35a5df872f100ca7e279926.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 4,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c18a864fecc0092e5be9451ffbc9e9e3bd5d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" />, that the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/255db3295cd47acafdddba42048037a6c4acf4ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2, 5,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/4/af467b8fe885cc26c6c6fa7d44c2eb574d0b9dac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" />, and that the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/c/a8c1c4d62e951f33cac6078ea46a7435b40bb907.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, 6,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/b/63b51d9295f2b809366efdc9d56520a1113be778.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" />. In order for a sum of three of these numbers to be a multiple of three, the mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> sum must be congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Quick inspection reveals that the only possible combinations are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/9/229e1f64893d1a8c108666db04dfb93ea7ee8350.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0+0+0, 1+1+1, 2+2+2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"235\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/3/af3d1cf2152b4a416c643e542634212c1a3a621a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0+1+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" />. However, every set of three consecutive vertices must sum to a multiple of three, so using any of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/1/8619529116ebceec2a0f808f9b0bf79cc8ba2de8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0+0+0, 1+1+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"150\" height=\"16\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/4/d74b62c40f97d7c5801cd4cbd6468cc6ebab6df1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+2+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"71\" height=\"13\" /> would cause an adjacent sum to include exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> digits with the same <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/f/7ffb54dae2d6238385a61cce8b447c54e319d733.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bmod{3}$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> value, and this is an unacceptable arrangement. Thus the only possible groupings are composed of three digits congruent to three different <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/f/7ffb54dae2d6238385a61cce8b447c54e319d733.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bmod{3}$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> values. \n</p><p>We see also that there are two possible arrangements for these trios on the nonagon: a digit congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c18a864fecc0092e5be9451ffbc9e9e3bd5d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" /> can be located counterclockwise of a digit congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and clockwise of a digit congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/4/af467b8fe885cc26c6c6fa7d44c2eb574d0b9dac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" />, or the reverse can be true. \n</p><p>We set the first digit as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> avoid overcounting rotations, so we have one option as a choice for the first digit.  The other two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/b/63b51d9295f2b809366efdc9d56520a1113be778.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" /> numbers can be arranged in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/3/46377b53287d0b7f76190da9e05acbd0fdedd334.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2!=2$\" width=\"47\" height=\"13\" /> ways.  The three <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c18a864fecc0092e5be9451ffbc9e9e3bd5d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"79\" height=\"18\" /> and three <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/4/af467b8fe885cc26c6c6fa7d44c2eb574d0b9dac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" /> can both be arranged in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/2/3920c5e3f6b2e5749cd75c3853b63ae714d00561.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" /> ways.  Therefore, the desired result is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df12306cb9380d43543911a34f0caacbd9b97e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2(2 \\times 6 \\times 6)=\\boxed{144}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"158\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that there are three triplets of congruent integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/ccca648bede4198fd656121337d515b0709e6280.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mod 3$\" width=\"61\" height=\"12\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/5/3650f73d13f1edde7262ca58603c9f000dfce575.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,4,7),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/7/c97ec7782fd012d88b0f5fc7a27ebdeb4fadf104.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,5,8),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/3/7d3945acdab061c8c0571893c9d868400745b849.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,6,9).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6859713adc973d3fbe97fafef9ce829757e54b12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> ways to order each of the triplets individually and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6859713adc973d3fbe97fafef9ce829757e54b12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> ways to order the triplets as a group (see solution 1). Rotations are indistinguishable, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/4/7f4b65da2833d66d2d6c0c846e19b42c45c87241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3!)^4/9=\\boxed{144}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"119\" height=\"25\" /> total arrangements.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Cyclic_Residues_and_Equivalencies.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Cyclic_Residues_and_Equivalencies)\">Solution 3 (Cyclic Residues and Equivalencies)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Cyclic Residues and Equivalencies)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Essentially, if we sum up each three consecutive vertices, we should see equivalencies modulo 3. To approach this, label the vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/1/f01d5683fa47c2479f62608cd4b2c9f97da3a457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b, c, \\dots, i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" /> in that order. Then, cyclical residues give\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/1/5c19895f0b3524722e0dbedf4c9a197837838fa8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a+b+c \\equiv b+c+d \\equiv c+d+e \\equiv \\dots \\equiv h+i+a \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}\\]\" width=\"512\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>From the cyclic orientation of all these residues, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/3/923529a7289f44223b5198a09594153e8e7391ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\equiv d \\equiv g \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"151\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/f/45f92b4b99f813627818d4d213ceaf2464dc4075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b \\equiv e \\equiv h \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"149\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/7/cd7eb1508d6499ba8b640e64a2aeeee739cde772.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c \\equiv f \\equiv i \\pmod{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"148\" height=\"20\" />. Residue three classes for each give three ordered pairs congruent modulo 3, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/5/3650f73d13f1edde7262ca58603c9f000dfce575.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,4,7),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/7/c97ec7782fd012d88b0f5fc7a27ebdeb4fadf104.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,5,8),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/2/ed217187af76600c14e059c7373329bb3721ef77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,6,9)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />. Each of these pairs can be given to each of the ordered <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/a/fcad5e0eea353e1a09a4d781d5c1becd3e10fbac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,d,g)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/2/e4238c4016f558f7ab1c463a415cfe24fb97eb70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(b,e,h)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/4/e34734ebedf0a39d0f9d54b25c4d3393ee56f0cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(c,f,i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" />. Each pair has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6859713adc973d3fbe97fafef9ce829757e54b12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> orderings, meaning there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/0/ac0ce1d90e551495feecdf489319c70a70ec8be5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3!)^3 = 216$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"89\" height=\"21\" /> total combinations with one placement. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/4/2a4df448919e7de0098afb1b9856646403d27182.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C =$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"18\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/a/fcad5e0eea353e1a09a4d781d5c1becd3e10fbac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,d,g)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/2/e4238c4016f558f7ab1c463a415cfe24fb97eb70.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(b,e,h)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/4/e34734ebedf0a39d0f9d54b25c4d3393ee56f0cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(c,f,i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" />, and an addition multiplicity of 6 is attributed to the previous 216 combinations, giving 1296 total. Each of these is not accounting for the multiplicity by the 9 rotations of the nonagon, and therefore there is a grand total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/f/02f4edb9607347a1f08b1022838eaef17c3fe2a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1296}{9} = \\boxed{144}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"105\" height=\"38\" /> distinct arrangements.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_solution\">Video solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vkniYGN45F4\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vkniYGN45F4</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I\" title=\"2011 AIME I\">2011 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2011 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2011 AIME I An